many are held ahead of it, and each held entry is named to the operator again every lap. That record is a position, never evidence — what an entry's deletion needs does not change with it, and one that names nothing of the upload's range is named to the operator and read as the start. It is written in a commit of its own, outside the pass's batch budget so that uploads with held entries cannot starve the rest, at most twice per upload a pass, put only while the upload's record is unchanged, and removed at the latest with the upload. Once a pass finds the range empty and no obligation of the upload left, one batch, conditioned on the upload's record exactly as read, deletes the record and the slots its parts reserved, deletes the reservation of its segment group unless a published object adopted the group, and lowers the admission count by one: of two drainers that race to it, one commits and the other's batch is refused, so the count goes down exactly once and an admission slot is released once. A **completed** upload's record is the tombstone that answers a retried completion with the published result, so it is kept, and its admission slot with it, until its tombstone window has passed; nothing measures that window yet, so the deployed custodian keeps every completed upload's record for now. For an **aborted** upload only, a record whose placement cannot be trusted — a part or a staging entry whose placement is not one server per fragment, or a staging entry that will not decode under a key that still names its chunk — is retired **by chunk id**: every fragment of that chunk that the D servers' own listings show gets a grace record, and for a staging entry that decodes so does every position it names, before the record goes. This runs only on a pass that sees every server the operator configured, the condition GC already runs under, since a listing that misses a server would leave that server's fragments with neither record nor grace record; on any other pass the record is held and named to the operator, and nothing is lost while it waits. The positions a record names itself are a fixed order, so the drain resumes inside them from its cursor across as many batches as they need. A listing is no such order — a fragment that drops out of it moves every later one — so a batch that reaches a chunk's listed fragments reads all of them, and progress is counted a whole chunk at a time; a chunk listed with more fragments than one batch reads is held and named to the operator as needing a human. A batch lists the fleet at most once, so a pass lists it at most once per batch it commits, plus once per aborted upload whose damaged record is held after its listing. One gap is stated rather than hidden: a write the aborted upload had authorized that lands after the listing, at a position the damaged record does not name, has neither record nor grace record, and GC keeps it — kept for good, never lost, never reclaimed without an operator. A part record that will not decode names no chunk in its key, so it is held for a human, and the upload stays until one acts. A grace record the walk wrote over a position whose write never landed stays as well, since that write may still land there.
 
