re reported either way, as they are when that last read fails. A first upload created under its own profile while the pass works is left for the next pass, and that pass reports that it did not finish rather than that nothing is left. It lists the uploads in key order, at most a fixed number per pass, and keeps where it stopped in a position of its own, `custodian:reaper-cursor`, so the next pass goes on from there and every upload is judged once a lap (a stored position that is not an upload key a backend accepts is named and replaced by the head, never handed to the store). When that number runs out it reads one entry further, judging none, so a listing that ends exactly there is known to have ended. A read of the listing the store cannot answer — a page of it, or that one entry — holds the rest of the listing for that pass, named for an operator, and sends the next pass back to the head rather than to the read that failed, so a fault that lasts never keeps the uploads listed before it from being judged again. The position also records whether the lap so far is clean — nothing ended, contended or held since its head — so the pass that finishes a clean lap reports that nothing is left, and one that finishes any other lap reports that it did not finish, and a longer listing still reaches the first report once a whole lap finds nothing to do; the admission count does not bound the listing, since a lowered limit or uploads created and deleted between two pages can list more uploads than the count, and neither is damage. It judges only an **open** one stamped under the clock it reads. An upload stamped under another clock is named for an operator and never judged; while it is open only an operator can end it, so it is held, and once it has ended its teardown runs as any other's, since none of it reads a timestamp. An upload whose creation or newest progress is stamped later than the reaper's own clock by more than the clock-skew budget is held too, rather than kept for